# Why does the default win?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What happened when firms made pension saving automatic? Why does doing nothing count as a choice?"
next: {"title":"Why do we put off what we planned to do?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/88-why-do-we-put-off-what-we-planned-to-do.cast"}

## One form, flipped
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Sign up to join
      - Auto-enrolled
    values:
      - 37
      - 86
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    colors:
      - grey
      - red
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 100
    y_label: In the plan after 3–15 months (%)
    gap: 0.5
    label_size: 26
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 150
      w: 520
      h: 480
sources:
    - id: ms2001
      title: 'The Power of Suggestion: Inertia in 401(k) Participation and Savings Behavior'
      authors: Brigitte C. Madrian, Dennis F. Shea
      year: 2001
      finding: 'A Fortune 500 health-care and insurance firm switched new hires to automatic enrolment (3% of pay, 100% money market fund) on 1 April 1998; match and funds unchanged. Participation at 3–15 months of tenure: 37% for the WINDOW cohort (opt-in) vs 86% for the NEW cohort (auto). 61% of the NEW cohort sat at the exact default vs about 1% of earlier cohorts. Quarterly Journal of Economics 116(4): 1149–1187.'

    icon firm size 110 x 800 y 470 icon_look picture of "office building" hidden true
    text flip "From April 1998:\nnew hires in at 3%\nunless they say no" x 800 y 470 font_size 36 hidden true
    text same "Same plan\nsame match\nsame funds" x 800 y 470 font_size 40 hidden true
    text x23 "×2.3" #b5482e cites ["ms2001"] x 800 y 470 font_size 72 hidden true
    scratch sc right cites ["ms2001"] work ["Default: 3% of pay,","all in money market","At exactly that:","new hires 61%","earlier hires 1%"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text cite "Madrian & Shea, 2001" #8f887c cites ["ms2001"] x 800 y 250 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "One form, flipped"

Why does the option you get for doing nothing so often end up being the one people have? Take one company, and its pension plan.
    draw axes

B: A large Americ (@draw firm@)an health insurer, a Fortune 500 firm. Its staff could save part of their pay in a 401(k), a tax-favoured retirement account.

    erase firm

Until April 1998, to join you had to fill in a form: yes, this much, in these funds. Of workers hired in the  (@draw bar_1@)year before, thirty-seven percent were in after three to fifteen months.

B: Then the firm flipped the f (@draw flip@)orm. New hires were signed up automatically, at three percent of pay, unless they filled in a form to say no.

    erase flip

Same plan, same tax break, same employer match, same funds. Only which box came ticked had changed.
    draw same

B: So, the economist's first answer: saving for retirement is a big decision. Nobody lets a pre-ticked box make it, so the share barely moves.
    highlight bar_1 effect pulse

    erase same

    ask "Drag the 'Auto-enrolled' bar to your guess." on bar_2 store g default "45"
        intro: Let's test that. How many of the auto-enrolled new hires were in the plan, at the same three to fifteen months?
        right: 86 percent: close to your {g}.
        wrong: You guessed {g} percent: it was 86 percent.

Eighty-six percent. More than double, from a  (@draw x23@)change that cost nothing and forced nothing.

    pause 0.5

    erase x23

B: And they didn't just stay in. They stayed put: three percent  (@draw sc_box sc_line_1 sc_line_2@)of pay, all of it in the money market fund, the safe default.

Sixty-one percen (@draw sc_line_3 sc_line_4@)t of the new hires sat at exactly that default. Among workers hired before the switch, a (@draw sc_line_5@)bout one percent had chosen that combination themselves.

    erase sc

B: Brigitte Madri (@draw cite@)an and Dennis Shea published it in 2001. The box that came ticked didn't just change who j (@highlight bar_2@)oined. It became what people saved.

    pause 0.5

## Why nothing is a choice
domain: {"x":[0,100],"y":[0,100]}
vars:
    want:
      value: 73
      min: 10
      max: 95
      step: 1
    act:
      value: 51
      min: 0
      max: 100
      step: 1
    yin:
      expr: want*act/100
    yout:
      expr: 100-(100-want)*act/100
    g:
      expr: yout-yin
sources:
    - id: ms2001
      title: 'The Power of Suggestion: Inertia in 401(k) Participation and Savings Behavior'
      authors: Brigitte C. Madrian, Dennis F. Shea
      year: 2001
      finding: 'Participation at 3–15 months: 37% under opt-in, 86% under automatic enrolment. Quarterly Journal of Economics 116(4): 1149–1187.'
    - id: sz1988
      title: Status Quo Bias in Decision Making
      authors: William Samuelson, Richard Zeckhauser
      year: 1988
      finding: 'In experiments and field data (health plans, retirement allocations) people stick with the status quo disproportionately often. Journal of Risk and Uncertainty 1(1): 7–59.'

    axes ax y_label "In the plan (%)" ticks.x [0,25,50,75,100] ticks.y [0,25,50,75,100] hidden true
    text xl "Act on the form (%)" at.data [50,-10] font_size 26 hidden true
    curve signup #2f6b8f expr want*x/100 hidden true
    curve auto #b5482e expr 100-(100-want)*x/100 hidden true
    label t_sign "Sign up" below-right #2f6b8f attach_to signup hidden true
    label t_auto "Auto" above #b5482e attach_to auto hidden true
    text wcue "Want in: ?" at.data [30,60] font_size 36 hidden true
    text wcue2 "Want in: {want:0}%" at.data [30,60] font_size 36 hidden true
    dot meet at.on signup at.x 100 hidden true
    text meetcue "Everyone acts:\ndefault irrelevant" at.data [80,93] font_size 28 hidden true
    dot pin #2f6b8f at.on signup at.x 51 bind {"at.x":"act"} hidden true
    dot pauto #b5482e at.on auto at.x 51 bind {"at.x":"act"} hidden true
    label pin_t "{yin:0}%" below-right #2f6b8f attach_to pin font_size 32 hidden true
    label pauto_t "{yout:0}%" above-right #b5482e attach_to pauto font_size 32 hidden true
    arrow gaparrow pin -> pauto #8a5fa8 hidden true
    label gapcue "never act: {g:0}" left #8a5fa8 attach_to gaparrow cites ["ms2001"] font_size 28 hidden true
    text sqb "Status quo bias" at.data [30,8] cites ["sz1988"] font_size 36 hidden true
    text why "Effort now\nLooks like advice\nGiving something up" at.data [27,62] font_size 28 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"skipmath"}] then skipmath hidden true
    scratch mcard work ["86 − 37 = 49 never act","→ 51% act","37 ÷ 51 ≈ 73% want in","100 − 0.27 × 51 ≈ 86 ✓"] x 795 y 240 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Why nothing is a choice"

Eighty-six against thirty-seven, for the same plan. To see how a tick box can do that, take the simplest model there is.
    draw ax xl

B: Say some share of workers would want to be in the plan if they thought it throu (@draw wcue@)gh. Call it the share who want in.

    erase wcue

And only some share ever acts on the form, in either direction (@highlight ax@). That share runs along the bottom, from nobody to everybody.

B: In the sign-up world you're in only if you want (@draw signup t_sign@) in and you act. So this line rises from zero.

In the auto world you're in unless you want ou (@draw auto t_auto@)t and you act. This line falls from a hundred.

B: They meet only at the far right. I (@draw meet@)f everyone acts, the form doesn't  (@draw meetcue@)matter: each person ends up where they wanted.

That's the textbook rati (@highlight meet effect circle@)onal chooser. A decision worth this much, so of course you act, and the default is irrelevant.

    erase meetcue

B: Now match the firm. Put about half acting, and about seventy-three percent wanti (@draw pin pauto pin_t pauto_t wcue2@)ng in, and the lines give thirty-seven and eighty-six.

So half the workers let the box decide. And the ga (@draw gaparrow gapcue@)p between the lines is exactly the share who never act, whatever they want.

B: William Samuelson and Richard Zeckhauser named this in 1988: status quo  (@draw sqb@)bias. Whatever is already in place gets chosen disproportionately often.

    erase wcue2

Why? Acting takes effort now. (@draw why@) A default set by your employer looks like advice. And once it's yours, leaving it feels like giving something up.

    erase why

    draw wcue2

B: So doing nothing is a choice, made for you by whoever wrote the default. Try it: dr (@highlight pin pauto@)ag a dot toward everyone acting and watch the gap close.

    explore params ["act","want"]

    erase wcue2

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: A short detour, if you like.

@math

    erase mathq

The two real numbers pin the model down. The gap, eighty-six minus thirty-seven, is forty-nine, so fifty-one percent act.
    draw mcard_box mcard_line_1 mcard_line_2

B: Thirty-seven percent in, out of fifty-one percent acting, means about seventy-three percent want in.
    draw mcard_line_3

And check the other side: a hundred minus twenty-seven percent of fifty-one is about eighty-six. It fits, for a toy.
    draw mcard_line_4

    erase mcard

@skipmath

    erase mathq

    draw wcue2

## Stuck or advised?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Hired before
      - Auto-enrolled
    values:
      - 10
      - 76
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    colors:
      - grey
      - red
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 100
    y_label: Savers at the 3% default (%)
    gap: 0.5
    label_size: 26
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 300
      w: 500
      h: 350
sources:
    - id: ms2001
      title: 'The Power of Suggestion: Inertia in 401(k) Participation and Savings Behavior'
      authors: Brigitte C. Madrian, Dennis F. Shea
      year: 2001
      finding: '76% of auto-enrolled participants contributed the 3% default vs around 10% of participants hired earlier; three-quarters of non-auto-enrolled participants contributed 6% or more (the 50% match applies to the first 6% of pay). The authors attribute the default effect to inertia and to the default being read as implicit advice. Quarterly Journal of Economics 116(4): 1149–1187.'
    - id: dwp2021
      title: 'Workplace pension participation and savings trends of eligible employees: 2009 to 2020'
      authors: Department for Work and Pensions
      year: 2021
      finding: Workplace pension participation of eligible private-sector employees in Great Britain rose from 42% in 2012, when automatic enrolment began, to 86% in 2020.
    - id: chetty2014
      title: 'Active vs. Passive Decisions and Crowd-Out in Retirement Savings Accounts: Evidence from Denmark'
      authors: Raj Chetty, John N. Friedman, Søren Leth-Petersen, Torben Heien Nielsen, Tore Olsen
      year: 2014
      finding: About 85% of Danes are passive savers who do not respond to tax subsidies; automatic employer or government pension contributions raise their total saving, with little offset in other accounts. Quarterly Journal of Economics 129(3).

    text ghost86 "In the plan: 86%" #8f887c cites ["ms2001"] x 800 y 600 font_size 30 style.opacity 0.5 hidden true
    text six "Hired before:\n¾ saved 6%+" cites ["ms2001"] x 800 y 440 font_size 34 hidden true
    text stuck "Stuck,\nor advised?" #8a5fa8 x 800 y 440 font_size 40 hidden true
    scratch sc cites ["dwp2021","chetty2014"] work ["UK, opt-out from 2012:","42% → 86% by 2020","Denmark: ~85%","passive savers"] x 770 y 440 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Stuck or advised?"

B: The default wins because about half of us never touch the form. That's good news only if the default is a good one.
    draw axes ghost86

Among savers hired before the switch, about one in (@draw bar_1@) ten had picked three percent. Three-quarters saved six p (@draw six@)ercent or more, enough to collect the full employer match.

B: Amo (@draw bar_2@)ng the auto-enrolled savers, seventy-six percent sat at three.

    erase six

So the same inertia that pu (@highlight bar_2@)lled thousands in held many of them below what they would likely have chosen, in a safe fund with low returns.

    erase ghost86

B: But are they  (@draw stuck@)stuck, or taking advice? A rate picked by your employer looks like a recommendation, and Madrian and Shea suspected both.

And if you know little about pensions, following a sensible recommendatio (@highlight stuck effect underline@)n is a perfectly reasonable choice. Doing nothing isn't always a mistake.

    erase stuck

    fade axes bar_1 bar_2 to 0.2

B: So how sure are we that defaults (@draw cons@) move behaviour? Consensus. Britain made workplace pensions opt-out from 2 (@draw sc_box sc_line_1 sc_line_2@)012, and among eligible private-sector workers, saving rose from forty-two to eighty-six percent by 2020.

And it isn't just money moved between pockets. In Denmar (@draw sc_line_3 sc_line_4@)k about eighty-five percent of people turned out to be passive savers, so automatic pension contributions raised their total saving.

B: Why defaults work, inertia, ad (@draw cont@)vice, or the feeling of giving something up, is still contested. And who should get to set them is a fight of its own.

    erase sc

    fade axes bar_1 bar_2 to 1

So why does the default win? Because choosing takes an act and the default takes none. For everyone (@highlight bar_2@) who never acts, the person who wrote the form has already chosen.

    pause 0.5

    erase cons cont

    quiz "A gym renews contracts automatically unless members cancel. Half the members never touch their settings; of the others, 40% want to keep going. Out of 100 members, about how many renew?"
        right: About 70: the 50 who never act, plus 20 of the 50 who do.
        wrong: Everyone who never acts stays on the default: here, about 70.
        * About 20
        * About 40
        + About 70

    quiz "A firm wants the pension default to matter as little as possible. Which design does that?" voice b
        right: The yes-or-no tick on day one. When everyone has to act, nobody is left on the default, and the gap closes.
        wrong: A default's power is the share who never act. Which design makes everyone act? Here, the yes-or-no tick.
        * A bigger employer match
        + Every new hire must tick yes or no on day one
        * A safer default fund

    quiz "A power company makes a slightly dearer green tariff the default, and most households stay on it. What does that alone show?"
        right: Not much. Staying is what doing nothing looks like, so on its own it reveals no preference.
        wrong: Staying put is what doing nothing looks like: here, not much.
        * They prefer green power
        + Not much: staying may be inertia or advice
        * They never saw the price

    text cons "Defaults move us: consensus" #3d3833 x 500 y 215 font_size 38
    text cont "Why: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 163 font_size 38

B: Which leaves the next puzzle:  (@highlight cont effect underline@)why don't we act, even when we fully mean to?

    pause 0.5

## Where next
end_page: true

